    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Well, right. And we are at a dangerous point in this country. The police officer who was responsible for the death is in prison right now, and I would surprise almost anybody if you not get a lengthy prisoner. The other police officers are also charged with something. You really don't have to raise awareness because this horrific video has been on the TV. I'm sure the average American has seen it dozens of times. So we do have a concern here, though, the huge amount of property crime that has happened, which is not going to turn around. I'm sure there are areas in Minneapolis and Chicago in particular, which is going to be a long time before anybody puts a store there. The huge number of people who died in Chicago Friday, Saturday, Sunday on that three-day period, there were 30 deaths. Normally in Chicago, there are maybe eight or nine deaths, 30 deaths, So maybe 20 unnecessary deaths caused by this free-for-all sort of atmosphere. I am thinking of someone who died in Milwaukee after the protests on Monday night, Tuesday morning, 4 in the morning. A guy working at an online gas station in Milwaukee was killed. You don't hear about him. As a matter of fact, I bought the newspaper the next day that he didn't put it in there, that this guy died. No celebration of his family or tracking down his children or tracking down his parents. It's just accepted. I don't know why it's accepted that way. But a lot of people have died and a lot of neighbors have been destroyed. I don't know if your listeners know this. I don't know if you know this. But when I was in the state legislature, I had the bill that would make it easier to let go of a bad policeman. And that bill didn't move in part because of the strength of the public employees. I'm with the police almost all the time, but there are a few lemons out there. And it would be good if we would change the law and make it easier to get rid of the So, I understand that. I did have a bill that the police union didn't want, but the police unions usually are very powerful and determined who the mayor is and, you know, who's on the city council, which is part of the problem. But these people better look out because if you don't have police, you have chaos. And some of these neighborhoods in Chicago or Milwaukee are going to hit chaos a lot quicker than say a neighborhood in Ashwabana. So not only when you defund the police, but when you create a situation in which the police are afraid to be active, you have a big increase of crying very quickly. And I am afraid, even though this isn't a congressional problem, it's a local problem, because it varies, you know, the degree to which they monitor the police from city to city. I'm a little afraid. that Congress will go overboard. They usually react to any crisis. They will react to the COVID that they will do something to make it very difficult to have police do their job. And if they do that, it will result in increasing murder rate around the country, increasing amount of crime around the country. I do think that people should pull out my bill from 2007, make it law on the state of Wisconsin today. But at the time, we weren't able to get it through. And quite frankly, historically, it's the Democrats who, you know, side with a policy of making it difficult to get rid of a bad employee. But the vast majority of police are good. And America better circle away and support our police. And if they don't support our police, we are going to find out what happens without police. with so much red tape and bureaucracy that police aren't able to do their job effectively and the results will be an increase of crime. And quite frankly, an increase in crime of the areas of the cities that already have the higher crime rates.",
